{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CORRECTION (operator): STOP DISCOVERING. We own the image — declare the contract and assert it.\n\nMy previous comment said \"verify, do not assume, at every step\" and treated\n`_discover_project_templates` as the model to follow. **That is wrong, and the operator is right:\nruntime discovery is a symptom of not owning the environment, not a defence against it.** We build\nthis image. We should know exactly what is in it.\n\n### What is wrong with discovering every run\n\n * **It hides drift instead of surfacing it.** If a future image ships a different template set,\n   discovery silently adapts and the suite quietly tests a DIFFERENT environment. That is precisely\n   how the missing `Epic` type reached three separate 35-minute runs without anyone being told.\n * **It makes every run non-deterministic** — the configuration under test is whatever the instance\n   happened to offer that morning, so a red cell can mean a product defect OR an environment\n   difference, and the suite cannot tell you which.\n * **It costs REST round trips per run** for an answer that is fixed at image-build time.\n * **The fallback chain is the bug in miniature**: `_create_project` degrades templates → bare\n   software → no template, takes the first success, and asserts nothing about the result. A silent\n   degrade is indistinguishable from a good provision.\n\nThe existing docstring's reasoning (\"hardcoding a template key does not survive contact with a real\ninstance\") diagnosed real pain, but drew the wrong conclusion. The fix for \"instances vary\" is to\nSTOP VARYING THE INSTANCE, not to adapt to whatever arrives.\n\n### The model this should move to\n\n 1. **PIN the image by digest**, not a floating tag. One known artifact, one known configuration.\n 2. **DECLARE the required environment as data** — the issue types, fields and screens the suite\n    needs — in one place a reader can see without running anything.\n 3. **CONFIGURE deterministically at setup**: create the project and add exactly the declared types\n    and screen fields. No template roulette, no fallback chain. If a step fails, fail — do not\n    substitute something weaker.\n 4. **ASSERT the declared contract ONCE at startup** and abort with the diff if reality differs.\n    This is the ONE place a read-back belongs: it is the drift detector, and it turns an image\n    change into an immediate, named failure instead of a mystery 35 minutes later in one cell.\n 5. **Better still, bake it into the image** at build time so setup only verifies. Discovery then\n    becomes a one-time AUTHORING activity — someone inspects the image once and writes the contract\n    down — rather than a per-run behaviour.\n\n### What this changes about the acceptance criteria\n\nSupersede \"every step reads the result back from the instance rather than assuming\" (my previous\ncomment) with: **the environment is declared and configured deterministically; exactly one\nassertion verifies the whole declared contract at startup, and any mismatch aborts the run.**\n\nKeep from the previous comment: the required capability set itself (issue types `Task`/`Sub-task`/\n`Epic`; `Epic Name` reachable on the Epic create screen; **`Epic Link` settable on a child's EDIT\nscreen** — the HTTP 400 \"not on the appropriate screen\" failure predicted on\n[rebar:39c1-2a32-b564-4b4b] is still queued behind the missing type and will bite next otherwise).\n\n### Worth noting for whoever picks this up\n\n`_discover_project_templates` and the `_create_project` fallback chain should be DELETED, not\nextended. Leaving them alongside a declared contract gives two sources of truth about the\nenvironment, and the adaptive one will win silently — the same reason 9bb9 insisted on a single\nshared Epic-Link discovery rather than a second parallel lookup."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785798025590551001,"uuid":"2cd18a99-e5c6-485b-ae31-05790b9bbb99"}